WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.426 --> 00:00:05.319
Hello, everyone! My name is Artem Vorozhtsov.

00:00:05.319 --> 00:00:08.629
And today I'm going to talk about Fibonacci numbers.

00:00:08.629 --> 00:00:12.662
It is impossible to tell the full story about Fibonacci numbers ,  I'll try to cover just tiny portion.

00:00:12.662 --> 00:00:13.643
So...

00:00:13.643 --> 00:00:20.686
Fibonacci numbers are numbers like: 1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 13, ... and so on.

00:00:20.686 --> 00:00:24.943
Each number is the sum of the two preceding ones.

00:00:24.943 --> 00:00:31.959
We've defined this explicitly with the expression F[n_] := F[n-1]+F[n-2];

00:00:31.959 --> 00:00:33.875
Will it work?

00:00:33.875 --> 00:00:35.657
No, it won't...

00:00:35.657 --> 00:00:37.941
Recursion depth exceeded...

00:00:37.941 --> 00:00:42.562
Because it doesn't know
what the first two Fibonacci numbers are.

00:00:42.562 --> 00:00:45.326
They are 1, 1.
We need to specify them.

00:00:45.326 --> 00:00:47.396
Let's be imperative programmers for a moment

00:00:47.396 --> 00:00:59.679
and define a function F1 that returns 1 if n=0 or n=1
(two vertical lines " || " mean "or").

00:00:59.679 --> 00:01:03.984
Otherwise, it returns F[n-1]+F[n-2].

00:01:03.984 --> 00:01:07.578
In other words, it recursively returns itself
with smaller parameters.

00:01:07.578 --> 00:01:09.833
Let's check if this works...

00:01:09.833 --> 00:01:12.735
Yes, it works. 10946.

00:01:12.735 --> 00:01:13.934
Moving on.

00:01:13.934 --> 00:01:16.662
Range [0, 20].

00:01:16.662 --> 00:01:25.822
Apply the function F1 to all these numbers:
0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and so on.

00:01:25.822 --> 00:01:31.632
Interestingly enough, the same result could be obtained from this "not working" definition.

00:01:31.632 --> 00:01:34.069
Just had to add two more lines.

00:01:34.069 --> 00:01:37.051
Like this.

00:01:37.051 --> 00:01:38.214
Let's check...

00:01:38.214 --> 00:01:39.158
Yes!

00:01:39.158 --> 00:01:41.304
It works.

00:01:41.304 --> 00:01:48.056
Programmers (imperative) might wonder:
What's the hack? How can these three lines work?

00:01:48.056 --> 00:01:52.349
They are defined independently of each other,

00:01:52.349 --> 00:01:56.452
and for some reason, the first line led to infinite recursion,

00:01:56.452 --> 00:02:02.059
but these two additional lines remove this infinite recursion.

00:02:02.059 --> 00:02:04.617
I'll explain it like this:

00:02:04.617 --> 00:02:11.169
You can think about it as polymorphism.
Symbol F represents two different objects.

00:02:11.169 --> 00:02:15.867
On one hand, it is function F (":=" rule),  and on the other hand, it is a dictionary ("=" rule).

00:02:15.867 --> 00:02:18.045
And Mathematica does the following:

00:02:18.045 --> 00:02:26.273
when an expression with F comes in, it first checks if there is already a ready answer in the dictionary  F,

00:02:26.273 --> 00:02:30.549
and if not, then it goes to function F[n_].

00:02:30.549 --> 00:02:38.108
Let's see what, for example, 
 F[n_] will be equal to if we write xxx here.

00:02:38.108 --> 00:02:40.668
The first definition worked.

00:02:40.668 --> 00:02:45.417
Note that it did not move on further...

00:02:45.417 --> 00:02:50.281
Or rather, it did move on further,
found the recursion and stopped.

00:02:50.281 --> 00:02:52.170
Good.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:53.611
Let's erase this.

00:02:53.611 --> 00:02:55.209
Now...

00:02:55.209 --> 00:02:58.712
The new F works just like F1.

00:02:58.712 --> 00:03:08.344
F[25] is computed in about half a second,

00:03:08.344 --> 00:03:16.454
and F1[25] is computed in just over a second.

00:03:16.454 --> 00:03:23.436
Thus, function F1 computes
25 Fibonacci numbers for over a second,

00:03:23.436 --> 00:03:29.387
while function F does it in half a second – that's a lot.

00:03:29.387 --> 00:03:35.897
To make the calculations more efficient,
let's add memorization.

00:03:35.897 --> 00:03:38.146
Memorization is done like this:

00:03:38.146 --> 00:03:49.214
We define the function F2 in a very similar way,
but add F2[n]= inside it.

00:03:49.214 --> 00:03:53.773
Here again, a colon equals " := " defines a function (SetDelayed-rule),

00:03:53.773 --> 00:04:00.000
while an equals sign " = " puts 
 the value of n into the dictionary F2 (Set-rule).

00:04:00.001 --> 00:04:04.923
So, the function F2[n] is defined as follows:

00:04:04.923 --> 00:04:12.806
We need to evaluate this expression, get a numerical result, and save it in the dictionary F2.

00:04:12.806 --> 00:04:15.966
Only then we return this value.

00:04:15.966 --> 00:04:18.782
Let's check...

00:04:18.782 --> 00:04:25.996
It's practically instantaneous,
we can replace it with 35 and get even more numbers.

00:04:25.996 --> 00:04:35.101
Let me illustrate this idea with an example where one symbol can be both a function and a dictionary.

00:04:35.101 --> 00:04:37.256
Using the symbol g.

00:04:37.256 --> 00:04:40.000
g[n_] is equal to n as a function.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:45.264
g[n_, a_] as a function of two variables is equal to n + a.

00:04:45.264 --> 00:04:51.827
And we also defined 3 values of g at three points.
We stored them in the dictionary g.

00:04:51.827 --> 00:04:56.704
Let's check...

00:04:56.704 --> 00:04:58.704
The first line worked here.

00:04:58.704 --> 00:05:00.758
The second line worked here.

00:05:00.758 --> 00:05:03.979
g[42]...

00:05:03.979 --> 00:05:05.299
Correct.

00:05:05.299 --> 00:05:06.913
g[b] = 3

00:05:06.913 --> 00:05:13.647
Interestingly, if we write b = 2 and evaluate g[b] again,

00:05:13.647 --> 00:05:15.865
we get a different result.

00:05:15.865 --> 00:05:24.308
The reason is that this line was executed 
 when " b " was an undefined symbol.

00:05:24.308 --> 00:05:31.186
And that's exactly how it was stored in the dictionary g:
g of the symbol b is equal to 3.

00:05:31.186 --> 00:05:38.791
Here, in square brackets, it's not the symbol "b", 
 but the number 2.

00:05:38.791 --> 00:05:44.012
g[a] = a, the first line of the definition is executed here.

00:05:44.012 --> 00:05:50.583
By the way, we can view all of these definitions 
 using the Definition function.

00:05:50.583 --> 00:05:56.366
And with the FullDefinition function, we can see more.

00:05:56.366 --> 00:05:58.967
First of all, we see all the definitions,

00:05:58.967 --> 00:06:04.272
and also he writes some additional definitions of symbols

00:06:04.272 --> 00:06:13.659
that appear in the right side of SetDelayed rule (to the right of the ":=").

00:06:13.659 --> 00:06:18.319
Let's check the status of our audience.

00:06:18.319 --> 00:06:19.806
Oh...

00:06:19.806 --> 00:06:22.407
It seems like brains have exploded.

00:06:22.407 --> 00:06:24.149
Well, let's be more careful.

00:06:24.149 --> 00:06:29.121
Moving on to the next point - Continued Fractions.

00:06:29.121 --> 00:06:37.692
A continued fraction is such a multi-story expression where arbitrary numbers can be used instead of ones.

00:06:37.692 --> 00:06:40.000
Instead of these ones.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.301
Well, instead of these ones too.

00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:45.718
Let's see what we get...

00:06:45.718 --> 00:06:48.749
1/2

00:06:48.749 --> 00:06:52.295
2/3

00:06:52.295 --> 00:06:55.841
3/5

00:06:55.841 --> 00:06:58.485
5/8

00:06:58.485 --> 00:07:00.227
Moving on...

00:07:00.227 --> 00:07:05.277
Let's define this multilevel fraction.

00:07:05.277 --> 00:07:07.749
How do we do that?

00:07:07.749 --> 00:07:15.200
Let me temporarily put the letter x here

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:25.617
and write what Fib[4] is equal to.

00:07:25.617 --> 00:07:29.513
Fib[4] is equal to this.

00:07:29.513 --> 00:07:34.863
If we remove x from here,

00:07:34.863 --> 00:07:38.709
it immediately calculates the entire expression as 5/8.

00:07:38.709 --> 00:07:40.902
5

00:07:40.902 --> 00:07:41.723
6

00:07:41.723 --> 00:07:43.318
Looks like it works.

00:07:43.318 --> 00:07:44.545
Okay.

00:07:44.545 --> 00:07:50.912
Let's calculate for the first 20 natural numbers.

00:07:50.912 --> 00:07:57.636
It's clear that this is the ratio of neighboring Fibonacci numbers.

00:07:57.636 --> 00:08:03.630
To get the numerator of these numbers, 
 we need to call the Numerator function.

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:07.648
Let's see how quickly Numerator works.

00:08:07.648 --> 00:08:12.354
Also fast.

00:08:12.354 --> 00:08:19.560
Although this is 10 times slower than the F2 function.

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:22.955
The next interesting topic.

00:08:22.955 --> 00:08:30.882
Another place where Fibonacci numbers appear is
in the partitioning of a rectangle into squares.

00:08:30.882 --> 00:08:39.141
Here. We now have an image
on which I can explain what I'm going to do.

00:08:39.141 --> 00:08:43.245
Let's say we have some rectangle.

00:08:43.245 --> 00:08:53.276
We will take the smaller of its sides 
 and draw a square attached to it from the inside.

00:08:53.276 --> 00:08:56.736
We get the remainder. The remainder is also a rectangle.

00:08:56.736 --> 00:09:01.055
We will find the smaller side in it
and attach a square to it in the same way.

00:09:01.055 --> 00:09:03.742
Let's assume that at the top.

00:09:03.742 --> 00:09:07.503
We will again get a rectangle.

00:09:07.503 --> 00:09:11.736
We will also find the smaller side in it
and draw a square.

00:09:11.736 --> 00:09:14.896
Here, it turned out that square, square, square...

00:09:14.896 --> 00:09:17.411
I cut off one square each time,

00:09:17.411 --> 00:09:20.484
because that's how I already chose.

00:09:20.484 --> 00:09:29.369
But if you take 27/8,
such a longer rectangle,

00:09:29.369 --> 00:09:33.688
then at first we will have to cut off three squares.

00:09:33.688 --> 00:09:37.363
Okay, we get such a vertical rectangle.

00:09:37.363 --> 00:09:39.492
So we cut off a square from the top...

00:09:39.492 --> 00:09:40.353
Not enough...

00:09:40.353 --> 00:09:42.353
Let's cut off one more...

00:09:42.353 --> 00:09:46.688
And now we have a lying rectangle.

00:09:46.688 --> 00:09:51.264
From it, we cut off a square on the right
and there are still two squares left.

00:09:51.264 --> 00:09:55.282
That's how it goes...

00:09:55.282 --> 00:09:59.051
If we write 8 here, nothing will change.

00:09:59.051 --> 00:10:03.413
8 is how many steps we need to take.

00:10:03.413 --> 00:10:07.346
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8

00:10:07.346 --> 00:10:10.000
The first one doesn't count, apparently.

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:15.201
Eight steps are enough, 
 so further increasing this number will not lead to anything.

00:10:15.201 --> 00:10:17.634
Let's go back up.

00:10:17.634 --> 00:10:18.879
So...

00:10:18.879 --> 00:10:25.002
The function SplitToSquares should split the rectangle,

00:10:25.002 --> 00:10:33.314
wich has the bottom left corner at x1, y1,
the top right corner at x2, y2,

00:10:33.314 --> 00:10:35.805
into n parts.

00:10:35.805 --> 00:10:40.000
Well, into n minus one square and what's left.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:42.129
So...

00:10:42.129 --> 00:10:46.453
We use Module and temporary variables a, b.

00:10:46.453 --> 00:10:54.208
If you want to write a complex function with local variables, use Module.

00:10:54.208 --> 00:10:58.579
If n = 0, that is, there are 0 steps left,

00:10:58.579 --> 00:11:02.425
then we take and return this rectangle as is.

00:11:02.425 --> 00:11:04.128
Yes, I know. Too many curly braces...

00:11:04.128 --> 00:11:10.708
Well, yes, because I want to return a list with one figure.

00:11:10.708 --> 00:11:15.378
The fact that it is a list is indicated by the curly braces.

00:11:15.378 --> 00:11:18.727
One figure is a list of pairs.

00:11:18.727 --> 00:11:23.261
These are the second set of curly braces and the third ones.

00:11:23.261 --> 00:11:31.145
Now if we have more steps left,
I want to return a list of more than one figure.

00:11:31.145 --> 00:11:37.740
I calculate side a, side b,
and finding out which side is smaller.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:41.044
a is the length of x2 - x1.

00:11:41.044 --> 00:11:42.700
b is the height.

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:54.407
If a&lt;b, meaning our rectangle is in portrait orientation,
then we need to cut off one square from the top.

00:11:54.407 --> 00:11:56.922
How do we cut off one square from the top?

00:11:56.922 --> 00:11:58.230
This square.

00:11:58.230 --> 00:12:06.057
Indeed, the height of this square is a,
because it's y2 minus this expression.

00:12:06.057 --> 00:12:11.077
And the width of this square is also a,
because it's x2 - x1.

00:12:11.077 --> 00:12:14.709
This is the square that we cut off.

00:12:14.709 --> 00:12:18.040
And we're left with a rectangle with these coordinates.

00:12:18.040 --> 00:12:19.568
Check it.

00:12:19.568 --> 00:12:23.243
We need to cut it into n-1 pieces.

00:12:23.243 --> 00:12:25.715
Checking...

00:12:25.715 --> 00:12:29.218
Cutting...

00:12:29.218 --> 00:12:37.788
I asked to cut the rectangle 13/8
and make 2 cuts, which resulted in 3 figures.

00:12:37.788 --> 00:12:44.684
And if you ask for more cuts to be made,
this is what we get.

00:12:44.684 --> 00:12:45.803
Okay.

00:12:45.803 --> 00:12:50.208
The Graphics function takes a list of directives as its argument.

00:12:50.208 --> 00:12:55.644
The first directive is Hue,
to select a color with these parameters.

00:12:55.644 --> 00:13:00.264
The second directive is Rectangle. Draw a Rectangle.

00:13:00.264 --> 00:13:04.798
I'll show you how to work with Hue.

00:13:04.798 --> 00:13:11.200
When you type Hue, it suggests you to select the color you need in the selector.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:15.133
Okay, click and the necessary parameters are substituted.

00:13:15.133 --> 00:13:22.372
Then you execute it and see the rectangle you need.

00:13:22.372 --> 00:13:26.931
Color scheme number 10, we'll use it.

00:13:26.931 --> 00:13:30.000
This is what I already showed you.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:36.428
Let's explicitly write that
the coordinates are equal to Fibonacci numbers.

00:13:36.428 --> 00:13:44.827
That is, we want to take a rectangle
whose sides are neighboring Fibonacci numbers,

00:13:44.827 --> 00:13:50.649
and divide it into squares. n - 1 squares.

00:13:50.649 --> 00:13:55.956
Let's check how it all works...

00:13:55.956 --> 00:14:02.897
I take it and get a list of squares.

00:14:02.897 --> 00:14:04.596
Next.

00:14:04.596 --> 00:14:10.848
I take Range[Length[sqs]]
(from one to the number of squares).

00:14:10.848 --> 00:14:15.080
And this becomes my variable i.

00:14:15.080 --> 00:14:16.994
i = #

00:14:16.994 --> 00:14:21.098
These parentheses here are the expression.

00:14:21.098 --> 00:14:24.730
It is denoted as a function of the hash.

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:29.693
But inside this expression, we immediately send the hash value to the i variable.

00:14:29.693 --> 00:14:34.012
And then we write not " # ", but " i ".

00:14:34.012 --> 00:14:38.889
It's more visual than using the hash everywhere.

00:14:38.889 --> 00:14:44.442
i is an index. Index of the square in the sqs array.

00:14:44.442 --> 00:14:51.556
We take a Rectangle with vertices equal to this square.

00:14:51.556 --> 00:14:52.334
Like this.

00:14:52.334 --> 00:15:01.287
And we take its color from the colors array,
which I calculated above.

00:15:01.287 --> 00:15:06.911
As a result, we get a list of pairs. A list of directives.

00:15:06.911 --> 00:15:08.367
Set this color.

00:15:08.367 --> 00:15:10.000
Draw a Rectangle.

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:13.140
We turn this list of pairs into just a list.

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:18.268
Flatten means to turn a list of lists into a list.

00:15:18.268 --> 00:15:22.888
It removes the inner brackets.

00:15:22.888 --> 00:15:26.692
We draw and get what we see on the screen.

00:15:26.692 --> 00:15:28.177
Good!

00:15:28.177 --> 00:15:34.214
By the way, here we have a proof of a very interesting theorem.

00:15:34.214 --> 00:15:41.539
The sum of the squares of Fibonacci numbers that are involved here equals the area of the entire rectangle.

00:15:41.539 --> 00:15:46.073
And the area of the rectangle is the product of adjacent Fibonacci numbers.

00:15:46.073 --> 00:15:49.785
So, we take the product of adjacent Fibonacci numbers,

00:15:49.785 --> 00:15:56.552
and it should be equal to the sum 
 of the squares of all previous Fibonacci numbers.

00:15:56.552 --> 00:15:57.908
Let's check...

00:15:57.908 --> 00:16:00.252
Here's the sum of the squares,

00:16:00.252 --> 00:16:02.080
and here's the product.

00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:06.550
The sum of squares from zero to the fourth,

00:16:06.550 --> 00:16:09.558
and the product of the fourth and fifth.

00:16:09.558 --> 00:16:11.343
They match!

00:16:11.343 --> 00:16:12.817
We can verify this...

00:16:12.817 --> 00:16:14.388
Here are the Fibonacci numbers.

00:16:14.388 --> 00:16:16.388
Here are their squares.

00:16:16.388 --> 00:16:20.492
The square is a magical function. It is Listable. It threads through the list.

00:16:20.492 --> 00:16:29.753
It threads through the list.
That is, when called on a list, we get a list 
 in which this function is applied to each element.

00:16:29.753 --> 00:16:32.983
I remind you what Accumulate is.

00:16:32.983 --> 00:16:40.652
Accumulate of a list is a list
consisting of the sum of various prefixes.

00:16:40.652 --> 00:16:43.405
Prefix is the beginning of a list.

00:16:43.405 --> 00:16:47.861
The beginning of the list "a",
The beginning of the list "a + b",
The beginning of the list "a + b + c".

00:16:47.861 --> 00:16:53.340
All possible prefixes are aggregated by summation.

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:59.572
Let's sum up all the prefixes of the squares 
 of Fibonacci numbers.

00:16:59.572 --> 00:17:02.001
This is what we will get.

00:17:02.001 --> 00:17:04.903
And now let's take the Fibonacci numbers...

00:17:04.903 --> 00:17:10.854
and take the Fibonacci numbers shifted by one and multiply these two lists.

00:17:10.854 --> 00:17:18.781
See? These Fibonacci numbers start at 1, 1, 2, and these start at 1, 2, 3.

00:17:18.781 --> 00:17:20.000
Multiplying:

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:22.344
1 * 1 = 1

00:17:22.344 --> 00:17:24.859
1 * 2 = 2

00:17:24.859 --> 00:17:27.937
2 * 3 = 6

00:17:27.937 --> 00:17:33.287
This is how you multiply two lists.
Simply place a multiplication sign between them.

00:17:33.287 --> 00:17:37.435
3 * 2 = 6

00:17:37.435 --> 00:17:42.854
3 * 5 = 15, everything matches

00:17:42.854 --> 00:17:46.787
5 * 8 = 40

00:17:46.787 --> 00:17:54.628
So, what do you think? Let's see your status...

00:17:54.628 --> 00:17:58.303
Okay... what is this?

00:17:58.303 --> 00:18:00.518
Alright. I'll be more careful.

00:18:00.518 --> 00:18:03.334
Now we're going to move on to a simpler topic.

00:18:03.334 --> 00:18:08.107
Oh, no.
It's not going to be simpler, it's just going to be more interesting.

00:18:08.107 --> 00:18:13.103
Let's immediately make sure
that all of our graphs have GridLines.

00:18:13.103 --> 00:18:19.698
I'll write that F = F2 and now,
hopefully, everything will be faster.

00:18:19.698 --> 00:18:21.011
Slap!

00:18:21.011 --> 00:18:23.244
There they are!

00:18:23.244 --> 00:18:26.446
A thousand Fibonacci numbers.

00:18:29.796 --> 00:18:32.088
A lot of digits...

00:18:32.088 --> 00:18:34.088
Oh, it didn't fit.

00:18:34.088 --> 00:18:35.232
Well, okay...

00:18:35.232 --> 00:18:36.242
We have to hide this somehow.

00:18:36.242 --> 00:18:38.242
There, it's hidden.

00:18:38.242 --> 00:18:43.753
Let's erase it and put a semicolon here
so that it doesn't output everything.

00:18:43.753 --> 00:18:46.913
Let it store the result in an array called fibs.

00:18:46.913 --> 00:18:50.215
And let's have it draw the logarithm of these numbers.

00:18:50.215 --> 00:18:55.723
I took the logarithm right away
because without it, things get really sad.

00:18:55.723 --> 00:19:01.492
If we remove the logarithm
and take the first 10 numbers, we get this image:

00:19:01.492 --> 00:19:04.780
But if we take the first 100 numbers, it looks like this.

00:19:04.780 --> 00:19:09.510
In general, it's meaningless to observe it.

00:19:09.510 --> 00:19:12.181
Everything is at the bottom, and then it goes up.

00:19:12.181 --> 00:19:16.243
Because Fibonacci numbers grow very quickly.

00:19:16.243 --> 00:19:18.485
And this can be seen in this plot.

00:19:18.485 --> 00:19:22.272
What is a logarithm? Perhaps not everyone knows.

00:19:22.272 --> 00:19:24.701
Essentially, it is the number of digits.

00:19:24.701 --> 00:19:27.690
We saw that the number of digits...

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:33.727
Ha! What do you think of that definition?
A logarithm is the number of digits.

00:19:33.727 --> 00:19:39.810
The Take function 
 takes a certain number of elements from an array,

00:19:39.810 --> 00:19:51.773
and it can be seen that the number of digits 
 grows with each step by a certain amount.

00:19:51.773 --> 00:19:53.336
Well, not with each step...

00:19:53.336 --> 00:20:01.478
Here we have a sequence of two-digit numbers, then a sequence of three-digit numbers, then a sequence of four-digit numbers...

00:20:01.478 --> 00:20:10.854
The point is that the number of numbers with the same number of decimal digits is roughly the same.

00:20:10.854 --> 00:20:18.086
Look again:
1, 2, 3, 4, 5,

00:20:18.086 --> 00:20:19.503
and there were 4.

00:20:19.503 --> 00:20:25.289
How many six-digit numbers will there be?
1, 2, 3, 4, 5

00:20:25.289 --> 00:20:33.926
How many seven-digit numbers will there be?
1, 2, 3, 4, 5

00:20:33.926 --> 00:20:37.018
This is logarithmic growth,

00:20:37.018 --> 00:20:48.045
when roughly the same number of steps are taken to add the next digit in some representation, such as decimal.

00:20:48.045 --> 00:20:51.106
A logarithm is...

00:20:51.106 --> 00:20:54.344
Let me give you some definition.

00:20:54.344 --> 00:21:00.338
Log[2, 8] = 3

00:21:00.338 --> 00:21:06.924
How many times do we need to multiply 2 by itself to get 8?

00:21:06.924 --> 00:21:09.579
Log[2, 16] = 4

00:21:09.579 --> 00:21:18.328
If we don't write 2 here, then we take some other number as the base, which is called "e".

00:21:18.328 --> 00:21:26.043
And we can numerically find out what Log[16] equals.

00:21:26.043 --> 00:21:27.497
Okay.

00:21:27.497 --> 00:21:31.754
The logarithm grows approximately linearly.

00:21:31.754 --> 00:21:36.012
Linearly means that you can put a ruller on your plot

00:21:36.012 --> 00:21:40.000
and see that they are approximately on a line.

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:45.749
Let's find this linear function.

00:21:45.749 --> 00:21:53.462
For this we use the function Fit - the simplest function 
 for regression.

00:21:53.462 --> 00:21:59.539
To find some approximate functions for your number series.

00:21:59.539 --> 00:22:04.162
0.48x

00:22:04.162 --> 00:22:12.868
We see that the Fibonacci numbers 
 are approximately a geometric progression.

00:22:12.868 --> 00:22:22.876
When the logarithm of the numbers grows linearly, it means that we are dealing with approximately a geometric progression.

00:22:22.876 --> 00:22:28.006
Let's find the power of this geometric progression.

00:22:28.006 --> 00:22:29.351
Approximately.

00:22:29.351 --> 00:22:32.491
For this, we need to divide adjacent 2 Fibonacci numbers.

00:22:32.491 --> 00:22:39.662
Well, we no longer have F2, we made it so that F = F2

00:22:39.662 --> 00:22:41.494
Here's the number...

00:22:41.494 --> 00:22:43.094
Oops...

00:22:43.094 --> 00:22:47.569
If there were some Gauss or Euler with us...

00:22:47.569 --> 00:22:51.594
he would say: Ah, I know this number!

00:22:51.594 --> 00:22:55.902
More precisely, he would say it in German...

00:22:55.902 --> 00:23:06.383
But in general, of course, any mathematician recognizes this number, as well as ancient Greek architects...

00:23:06.383 --> 00:23:10.000
This is the number φ "phi" - the Golden Ratio.

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:14.162
Let's write more digits.

00:23:14.162 --> 00:23:20.000
1.618033988...

00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:22.582
Remember it.

00:23:22.582 --> 00:23:25.583
And let's draw 2 graphs now.

00:23:25.583 --> 00:23:27.765
Our original graph...

00:23:27.765 --> 00:23:30.462
and here is the result of fitting.

00:23:30.462 --> 00:23:33.517
Fitting is the same as adjustment.

00:23:33.517 --> 00:23:38.320
We can draw it like this in different colors.

00:23:38.320 --> 00:23:43.378
Let's draw the dots in blue and the line in red.

00:23:43.378 --> 00:23:48.581
Now let's do something that's typical for researchers.

00:23:48.581 --> 00:23:55.875
Let's try to study these deviations.
Microscopic deviations from the red line.

00:23:55.875 --> 00:23:59.294
At first, they're big and then they get smaller and smaller.

00:23:59.294 --> 00:24:14.952
To do this, we need to subtract the approximate value of the logarithm of the Fibonacci numbers from the list of true values of the logarithm of the Fibonacci numbers.

00:24:14.952 --> 00:24:19.609
The approximate values look like this, I remind you.

00:24:19.609 --> 00:24:23.829
Where x is n.

00:24:23.829 --> 00:24:29.797
And instead of x, we substitute Range[100],
which is the first 100 natural numbers.

00:24:29.797 --> 00:24:33.332
And we get...

00:24:33.332 --> 00:24:37.916
We've fitted. It's clear that the difference in logarithms is small.

00:24:37.916 --> 00:24:43.119
At first, it jumps around, then it goes here.

00:24:43.119 --> 00:24:52.888
Honestly, it's not very good. Because then it will go away.

00:24:52.888 --> 00:24:57.399
Yes, it's not very good further.

00:24:57.399 --> 00:25:02.493
That is, it fitted it, but only in the vicinity of [0, 100].

00:25:02.493 --> 00:25:08.716
But in general, this fitting works poorly at infinity, it goes down.

00:25:08.716 --> 00:25:17.414
It's visible. And further, most likely, 
 it takes arbitrarily small values.

00:25:17.414 --> 00:25:25.700
Okay, but now we'll take not the result of the fitting, but directly the logarithm of φ.

00:25:25.700 --> 00:25:26.753
Slap!

00:25:26.753 --> 00:25:29.554
Oh! That's different!

00:25:29.554 --> 00:25:31.703
A smooth curve.

00:25:31.703 --> 00:25:35.013
He knows φ with absolute accuracy.

00:25:35.013 --> 00:25:47.023
And here we used such a trick:
that if you subtract one list from another, 
 you get the difference of the lists.

00:25:47.023 --> 00:25:50.552
A list of differences.

00:25:50.552 --> 00:25:58.303
Now let's add the constant 0.3235 that we have obtained.

00:25:58.303 --> 00:26:01.249
More precisely, we need to do the following:

00:26:01.249 --> 00:26:05.761
Add 0.32 -&gt; undershoot, cause we have negative numbers.

00:26:05.761 --> 00:26:11.292
Okay, let's write 0.325 here -&gt;positive numbers,  it is overshoot.

00:26:11.292 --> 00:26:19.080
Okay, so we write 0.324 -&gt; still overshoot...

00:26:19.080 --> 00:26:21.408
0.323 -&gt; undershoot.

00:26:21.408 --> 00:26:24.209
So between three and four...

00:26:24.209 --> 00:26:28.399
I write 0.3235.

00:26:28.399 --> 00:26:31.600
0.3235 - this is an undershoot.

00:26:31.600 --> 00:26:35.893
0.3237 - overshoot.

00:26:35.893 --> 00:26:42.371
0.3236, 0.3235.

00:26:42.371 --> 00:26:47.931
Between 5 and 6, so I write 55.

00:26:47.931 --> 00:26:52.151
Next, 55 -&gt; overshoot.

00:26:52.151 --> 00:26:55.825
I write 4...

00:26:55.825 --> 00:26:59.026
I write 3...

00:26:59.026 --> 00:27:01.790
I write 2...

00:27:01.790 --> 00:27:05.974
I write 1...

00:27:05.974 --> 00:27:10.000
Still the same. I write 0...

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:12.194
There. Okay.

00:27:12.194 --> 00:27:16.450
I write 5 here -&gt; undershoot.

00:27:16.450 --> 00:27:19.688
I write 7 here -&gt; undershoot.

00:27:19.688 --> 00:27:24.345
I write 8 here -&gt; overshoot.

00:27:24.345 --> 00:27:26.818
So between 7 and 8.

00:27:26.818 --> 00:27:33.914
Writing 75 -&gt; overshoot.

00:27:33.914 --> 00:27:40.392
Writing 73... 71

00:27:40.392 --> 00:27:45.972
71 - undershoot. So the next number is 5.

00:27:45.972 --> 00:27:53.250
Gradually, we are learning the truth about this constant.

00:27:53.250 --> 00:27:58.728
Even better than what fitting gave us earlier.

00:27:58.728 --> 00:28:02.131
Now Mathematica is starting to fail.

00:28:02.131 --> 00:28:08.241
So let's pause. Here is the constant.

00:28:11.335 --> 00:28:20.615
So we have calculated that Fibonacci numbers are approximately a geometric progression with a base of φ.

00:28:20.615 --> 00:28:26.073
The golden ratio is approximately 1.62.

00:28:26.073 --> 00:28:27.927
But there's a small correction:

00:28:27.927 --> 00:28:31.604
This number, which we put 
 under an exponent with a minus sign,

00:28:31.604 --> 00:28:37.281
because the truth minus this thing,

00:28:37.281 --> 00:28:43.431
plus this constant, means that actually this constant is not with plus, but with minus.

00:28:43.431 --> 00:28:44.945
Alright.

00:28:44.945 --> 00:28:48.219
Here is the approximate value of Fibonacci numbers.

00:28:48.219 --> 00:28:52.512
Here are the first ten approximate Fibonacci numbers.

00:28:52.512 --> 00:28:55.713
Approximately 1, approximately 2, approximately 3, approximately 5,

00:28:55.713 --> 00:29:00.515
approximately 8, approximately 13, approximately 21, approximately 34.

00:29:00.515 --> 00:29:01.969
That's what we need!

00:29:01.969 --> 00:29:10.375
Round - a function that rounds to the nearest integer. 
 Gives the truth.

00:29:10.375 --> 00:29:14.943
Oh, by the way, let's mark that all our F functions are Listable,

00:29:14.943 --> 00:29:22.527
so that we can create the same miracles with them as we do with logarithms, rounds, and so on.

00:29:22.527 --> 00:29:28.521
Round of a list equals: a list in which each element is rounded.

00:29:28.521 --> 00:29:32.239
Same with logarithms...

00:29:32.239 --> 00:29:35.356
And we want our function F to work the same way.

00:29:35.356 --> 00:29:39.031
To do this, we set the Listable attribute for the function F.

00:29:39.031 --> 00:29:44.728
And now we can write F[Range[20]] and get the answer for the whole range.

00:29:44.728 --> 00:29:46.556
Good!

00:29:46.556 --> 00:29:57.742
Now let's look at the errors that occur between our approximation and the true function F.

00:29:57.742 --> 00:30:04.362
They are small and seem 
 to be approaching zero in absolute value.

00:30:04.362 --> 00:30:07.049
Excellent!

00:30:07.049 --> 00:30:13.080
By the way, now that we understand that the Fibonacci numbers are approximately a geometric progression,

00:30:13.080 --> 00:30:19.570
let's look for geometric progressions that exactly satisfy this relationship.

00:30:19.570 --> 00:30:25.199
We write "=", and get this equation.

00:30:25.199 --> 00:30:31.073
We can see that this can be reduced by m. We reduce it.

00:30:31.073 --> 00:30:34.364
And this is what we get.

00:30:34.364 --> 00:30:37.609
This can be simplified to obtain an equation.

00:30:37.609 --> 00:30:40.211
This is actually a quadratic equation.

00:30:40.211 --> 00:30:44.315
If you mentally multiply by λ, you'll see why it's quadratic.

00:30:44.315 --> 00:30:46.787
Let's solve this quadratic equation.

00:30:46.787 --> 00:30:50.634
Oh, we'll find out. This is what φ is.

00:30:50.634 --> 00:30:52.634
And what is this?

00:30:52.634 --> 00:31:00.980
I'll tell you a secret, the second number is "-1/φ".

00:31:00.980 --> 00:31:08.790
This is exactly equal to this second λ.

00:31:12.612 --> 00:31:16.461
Yes. True.

00:31:16.461 --> 00:31:32.594
The numerical solution to the equation looks like this: λ = -0.618034 and 1.618034

00:31:32.594 --> 00:31:36.527
Let's check...

00:31:36.527 --> 00:31:39.586
We need to simplify. FullSimplify will help us.

00:31:39.586 --> 00:31:40.713
True.

00:31:40.713 --> 00:31:43.733
We're beginning to see elements of paranoid programming.

00:31:43.733 --> 00:31:45.389
True.

00:31:45.389 --> 00:31:46.616
Is that right?

00:31:46.616 --> 00:31:49.733
True.

00:31:49.733 --> 00:31:51.285
Moving on.

00:31:51.285 --> 00:31:57.193
We've found two geometric progressions, two different λs,

00:31:57.193 --> 00:32:02.929
that fit the geometric progression pattern,

00:32:02.929 --> 00:32:12.058
so that the relation c[n] = c[n-1] + c[n-2] holds.

00:32:12.058 --> 00:32:21.669
By the way, the sum of two geometric progressions will also satisfy this recurrence relation.

00:32:21.669 --> 00:32:25.215
Proof:
These three lines.

00:32:25.215 --> 00:32:29.742
If the first line is true and the second line is true,
then the third line is also true,

00:32:29.742 --> 00:32:35.693
because the third line is the sum of the first two equalities.

00:32:35.693 --> 00:32:42.368
Let's try to find such a and b that...

00:32:42.368 --> 00:32:45.534
the zeroth element of this sum of geometric progressions

00:32:45.534 --> 00:32:50.285
and the first element are both equal to 1.

00:32:50.285 --> 00:32:53.144
That is, like the first two Fibonacci numbers.

00:32:53.144 --> 00:32:55.788
The answer is here.

00:32:55.788 --> 00:32:58.046
Let's write it as...

00:32:58.046 --> 00:33:00.991
" a " times the first geometric progression

00:33:00.991 --> 00:33:04.666
plus " b " times the second geometric progression.

00:33:04.666 --> 00:33:11.519
This is another explicit formula for the Fibonacci numbers.

00:33:11.519 --> 00:33:14.639
Let's check what it gives...

00:33:14.639 --> 00:33:16.599
Wow...

00:33:16.599 --> 00:33:18.924
And numerically...

00:33:18.924 --> 00:33:22.470
Numerically, it seems to be true.

00:33:22.470 --> 00:33:23.783
Now FullSimplify.

00:33:23.783 --> 00:33:32.912
FullSimplify is able to collapse this expression to completely accurate integers.

00:33:32.912 --> 00:33:38.171
And the F3 execution time is also microscopic.

00:33:38.171 --> 00:33:40.171
All right.

00:33:40.171 --> 00:33:41.823
Moving on.

00:33:41.823 --> 00:33:44.424
It's a complex topic, though.

00:33:44.424 --> 00:33:49.040
Let's draw the generating function for Fibonacci numbers.

00:33:49.040 --> 00:33:51.512
So, here are our Fibonacci numbers.

00:33:51.512 --> 00:33:56.432
And now, let's take powers of 1, x, x², and so on.

00:33:56.432 --> 00:33:59.161
And let's multiply them element-wise.

00:33:59.161 --> 00:34:03.523
This is done like this, as described here.

00:34:03.523 --> 00:34:06.425
We need to multiply and then add.

00:34:06.425 --> 00:34:13.150
" Plus @@ ", and to the right of " @@ " should be a list.

00:34:13.150 --> 00:34:21.025
In this list, commas are replaced by the operator Plus, which is to the left of " @@ ".

00:34:21.025 --> 00:34:24.857
So, we get...

00:34:24.857 --> 00:34:32.748
We element-wise multiplied the top and bottom lists up to 10.

00:34:32.748 --> 00:34:37.797
And we got the following polynomial of degree 10.

00:34:37.797 --> 00:34:41.816
This is the generating function for Fibonacci numbers.

00:34:41.816 --> 00:34:47.724
Well, if we continue to infinity, 
 then this will be the generating function,

00:34:47.724 --> 00:34:50.797
but we took the first 10 elements.

00:34:50.797 --> 00:34:54.379
Let's pay attention to the next point.

00:34:54.379 --> 00:34:59.686
If we add the generating function
with x multiplied by itself,

00:34:59.686 --> 00:35:02.502
we'll get this thing.

00:35:02.502 --> 00:35:04.244
And what will happen?

00:35:04.244 --> 00:35:06.934
What will happen with x⁵, for instance?

00:35:06.934 --> 00:35:13.912
For x⁵, we'll have 8x⁵ plus...

00:35:13.912 --> 00:35:17.286
and from the second bracket comes out a five.

00:35:17.286 --> 00:35:21.763
5x⁴ multiplied by x, which will be 5x⁵,

00:35:21.763 --> 00:35:27.500
and 8 will be added to 5 to get 13.

00:35:27.500 --> 00:35:36.345
It will be 13. To do this, we need to execute the Expand function...

00:35:36.345 --> 00:35:42.210
Again, we get the generating function for Fibonacci numbers.

00:35:42.210 --> 00:35:47.082
Only it's shifted.

00:35:47.082 --> 00:35:58.402
Here we have 13x⁵, and before it was 13x⁶.

00:35:58.402 --> 00:36:02.764
So it turns out that we've divided by x.

00:36:02.764 --> 00:36:06.568
Let's write this down...

00:36:06.568 --> 00:36:10.000
We subtract 1 from g10 and divide by x.

00:36:10.000 --> 00:36:14.018
And we get this equation.

00:36:14.018 --> 00:36:19.110
Let's forget about the ten and imagine that it's an infinite series,

00:36:19.110 --> 00:36:20.831
and then the following identity will be true.

00:36:20.831 --> 00:36:23.475
Let's call the Solve function.

00:36:23.475 --> 00:36:26.763
Although it's not that hard to solve this mentally.

00:36:26.763 --> 00:36:36.021
We've obtained some function g[x], which is exactly this infinite series.

00:36:36.021 --> 00:36:44.334
Let's ask Mathematicia to compute this infinite series.

00:36:44.334 --> 00:36:48.751
There we go. We didn't make a mistake.

00:36:48.751 --> 00:36:55.382
By the way, note that from school math we know that

00:36:55.382 --> 00:37:02.493
1/(1-q)=1+q²+q³, etc.

00:37:02.493 --> 00:37:11.290
The sum of a geometric series, if it goes to infinity, is equal to this expression.

00:37:11.290 --> 00:37:14.393
But here we have x + x² as q.

00:37:14.393 --> 00:37:18.736
Look, here we have something like one minus q, and q is x + x²,

00:37:18.736 --> 00:37:21.766
so we can write it like this.

00:37:21.766 --> 00:37:24.300
Interesting!

00:37:24.300 --> 00:37:30.416
Let's remember what (a+b)², (a+b)³, (a+b)⁴, etc. are equal to.

00:37:30.416 --> 00:37:35.423
Let's draw Pascal's triangle.

00:37:35.423 --> 00:37:44.292
And let's think about what is in this expression on the right side when x⁶ is present.

00:37:44.292 --> 00:37:52.372
In this Pascal's triangle, we have y = x², and x = x.

00:37:52.372 --> 00:38:01.960
So from this bracket (x + x²)⁶, we get x⁶,

00:38:01.960 --> 00:38:06.021
and all the other terms are not interesting to us.

00:38:06.021 --> 00:38:11.500
Right now, we are interested in the coefficient of x⁶.

00:38:11.500 --> 00:38:23.211
In this bracket, there will be a term x⁶.

00:38:25.083 --> 00:38:31.138
Oh, it's in this bracket here.

00:38:31.138 --> 00:38:40.461
This term 5x⁴y will be in this bracket. 
 y = x². This gives us 5x⁶.

00:38:40.461 --> 00:38:46.884
Here we will find x⁶...

00:38:46.884 --> 00:38:48.283
at this point.

00:38:48.283 --> 00:38:54.234
x²y², y = x²
This gives us x²x⁴,

00:38:54.234 --> 00:38:57.264
and here we also get six...

00:38:57.264 --> 00:39:03.602
And in the previous line, x⁶ is found here - y³.

00:39:03.602 --> 00:39:08.222
So, to get x⁶, we need to take:

00:39:08.222 --> 00:39:10.780
this term,

00:39:10.780 --> 00:39:14.459
this term,

00:39:14.459 --> 00:39:16.846
this term,

00:39:16.846 --> 00:39:20.993
and this term.

00:39:20.993 --> 00:39:27.205
The entire Pascal's triangle, all the terms shown here, are present on the right side.

00:39:27.205 --> 00:39:36.935
Because on the right side, we see the sum of all the rows of Pascal's triangle.

00:39:36.935 --> 00:39:45.995
And we've just shown that for x⁶, it will be 1 + 5 + 6 + 1

00:39:45.995 --> 00:39:50.000
Here, I've written it down.

00:39:50.000 --> 00:39:57.797
That is, the sum of the binomial coefficients
(which are the coefficients in this triangle),

00:39:57.797 --> 00:40:04.521
that go diagonally like this, skipping every other one,

00:40:04.521 --> 00:40:11.202
the sum of these coefficients is equal to the Fibonacci number.

00:40:11.202 --> 00:40:15.593
You can check this, for example, for the previous x⁵.

00:40:15.593 --> 00:40:22.747
1 + 4 + 3

00:40:22.747 --> 00:40:23.667
Here there is no

00:40:23.667 --> 00:40:33.008
1 + 4 + 3 = 8
Indeed, the previous Fibonacci number is 8.

00:40:33.008 --> 00:40:36.511
Moving on...

00:40:39.130 --> 00:40:48.261
Let's note that if we replace the minus sign with a plus sign, we will get the same series, but alternating in sign.

00:40:48.261 --> 00:40:49.574
Interesting!

00:40:49.574 --> 00:40:54.538
What if we now multiply these two expressions?

00:40:54.538 --> 00:40:59.520
Here's what we get.

00:40:59.520 --> 00:41:01.136
We can notice the following:

00:41:01.136 --> 00:41:10.000
that the first expression is (1-x²)-x,
and the second expression is (1-x²)+x.

00:41:12.405 --> 00:41:16.012
We get that this is the square of the first 
 minus the square of the second.

00:41:16.012 --> 00:41:22.307
And then a little trick: subtract one and add one.

00:41:24.626 --> 00:41:33.312
This is very similar to what happened
if we take 1-x² as "y"

00:41:35.843 --> 00:41:42.580
Let's see what the expansion of this product is...

00:41:42.580 --> 00:41:47.028
and in this expansion, we will recognize the Fibonacci numbers.

00:41:47.028 --> 00:41:49.285
What does this imply?

00:41:49.285 --> 00:41:53.088
We've just had an interesting observation.

00:41:53.088 --> 00:41:58.696
To understand what this implies, we need to learn how to multiply two generating functions.

00:41:58.696 --> 00:42:02.214
Let's say we have two generating functions 
 with some coefficients.

00:42:02.214 --> 00:42:07.693
One with coefficients "a", and the other with coefficients "b".

00:42:07.693 --> 00:42:14.223
Let's think about what will happen at x⁶ when we multiply the first one by the second one.

00:42:14.223 --> 00:42:22.150
Firstly, x⁶ will be obtained when we multiply a[0] by x⁶b[6].

00:42:22.150 --> 00:42:29.003
Secondly, we can multiply xa[1] by x⁵b[5].

00:42:29.003 --> 00:42:37.617
Thirdly, we can multiply x³a[3] by x³b[3].

00:42:37.617 --> 00:42:42.924
Fourthly, x⁴a[4] can be multiplied by x²b[2].

00:42:42.924 --> 00:42:46.470
And so we get...

00:42:46.470 --> 00:42:49.742
that at x⁶, we get this expression.

00:42:49.742 --> 00:42:58.313
This expression is called the convolution
of the first 6 elements of the series "a",
and the first 6 elements of the series "b".

00:43:00.203 --> 00:43:07.141
We are multiplying two generating functions here,

00:43:07.141 --> 00:43:11.288
the first of which has coefficients "a" -
these are Fibonacci numbers,

00:43:11.288 --> 00:43:21.718
and the second of which has coefficients that are Fibonacci numbers with alternating plus and minus signs.

00:43:21.718 --> 00:43:24.974
So, we write it like this...

00:43:24.974 --> 00:43:35.048
At x⁶ we will have
F[0] * F[6] - F[1] * F[5] + F[2] * F[4] - F[3] * F[3] and so on.

00:43:36.079 --> 00:43:37.116
Let's calculate...

00:43:37.116 --> 00:43:40.705
21 is a Fibonacci number, I recognize it.

00:43:40.705 --> 00:43:45.926
And if we take the same convolution for the first five, we get 0.

00:43:45.926 --> 00:43:46.508
So...

00:43:46.508 --> 00:43:52.588
We can write all of this in the form of a table F[n-k] * F[k].

00:43:52.588 --> 00:44:00.000
And here comes the alternating sign.

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:07.165
Let's calculate the convolution function of Fibonacci numbers

00:44:07.165 --> 00:44:10.711
with the alternating sign sequence of Fibonacci numbers.

00:44:11.698 --> 00:44:15.229
We get a remarkable result.

00:44:15.229 --> 00:44:23.370
We see Fibonacci numbers, 
 in which some are missing, replaced by zeros.

00:44:23.370 --> 00:44:27.356
In essence, we have proved a certain identity.

00:44:27.356 --> 00:44:38.948
The convolution of Fibonacci numbers with itself and alternating signs is equal to Fibonacci numbers again.

00:44:38.948 --> 00:44:40.000
So...

00:44:40.000 --> 00:44:42.687
Next it's more difficult...

00:44:44.405 --> 00:44:48.071
Derivatives are being checked...

00:44:48.071 --> 00:44:53.421
Let's take the derivative of our generating function

00:44:53.421 --> 00:44:55.550
By the way...

00:44:55.550 --> 00:44:58.151
How are our viewers doing?

00:44:59.354 --> 00:45:00.927
Smoking...

00:45:00.927 --> 00:45:03.340
Smoking nervously...

00:45:03.340 --> 00:45:08.088
Okey, not nervous. The look is quite positive.

00:45:08.088 --> 00:45:10.515
Great!

00:45:10.515 --> 00:45:12.300
This is wonderful!

00:45:12.300 --> 00:45:18.252
The derivative of our generating function is this,

00:45:18.252 --> 00:45:21.850
and the square is this.

00:45:21.850 --> 00:45:26.856
They are similar in some way. 
The denominator of both is a square.

00:45:26.856 --> 00:45:28.681
Let's take the first one...

00:45:28.681 --> 00:45:32.399
Ah! Note that the first one has -2x.

00:45:32.399 --> 00:45:35.387
Or rather, +2x, because there's a minus sign in front.

00:45:35.387 --> 00:45:40.489
Let's subtract 2x from G' to get rid of this 2x.

00:45:40.489 --> 00:45:42.403
What will we get?

00:45:42.403 --> 00:45:43.372
Oh!

00:45:43.372 --> 00:45:44.906
We'll get something very simple.

00:45:44.906 --> 00:45:47.679
This is just the square of G, let's write it down.

00:45:47.679 --> 00:45:54.102
G'[x] - 2xG[x]² == G[x]²

00:45:54.102 --> 00:46:02.592
This is a differential equation 
 that the generating function G satisfies.

00:46:05.418 --> 00:46:06.870
Checking...

00:46:06.870 --> 00:46:09.324
G[x]²

00:46:09.324 --> 00:46:15.147
G[x]² yields:
1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 38...

00:46:15.147 --> 00:46:17.448
And if we take 1+2x,

00:46:17.448 --> 00:46:20.436
1, 4, 9, 20, 40...

00:46:20.436 --> 00:46:24.068
Here's G',

00:46:24.068 --> 00:46:25.556
Here's G prime,
1, 4, 9, 20, 40

00:46:25.556 --> 00:46:27.688
It matches!

00:46:27.688 --> 00:46:33.384
So it is indeed true that
(1+2x)G[x]² = G'[x]

00:46:33.384 --> 00:46:36.415
But what does this imply?

00:46:37.274 --> 00:46:38.887
It implies the following:

00:46:38.887 --> 00:46:49.562
G[x]² yields the Fibonacci convolution coefficients with itself, without alternating signs.

00:46:49.562 --> 00:46:53.899
For example, one term will be 38.

00:46:53.899 --> 00:46:57.395
And another term will be 71.

00:46:57.395 --> 00:47:03.389
And it says that if we add these two series,

00:47:03.389 --> 00:47:05.389
we will get something interesting.

00:47:05.389 --> 00:47:06.444
Let's check.

00:47:06.444 --> 00:47:14.328
So first let's define the Fibonacci 
 convolution without alternating signs.

00:47:14.328 --> 00:47:21.578
Let's write it as Listable,
so we can perform ConF on a list.

00:47:21.578 --> 00:47:26.359
(38, 71)
That's what we need. That's what we got above.

00:47:26.359 --> 00:47:32.062
Now let's multiply the numbers N by the Fibonacci numbers.

00:47:32.062 --> 00:47:33.977
So the first Fibonacci number times 1.

00:47:33.977 --> 00:47:37.952
The second Fibonacci number times 2,

00:47:37.952 --> 00:47:40.768
The third Fibonacci number times 3,

00:47:40.768 --> 00:47:45.517
The fourth Fibonacci number times 4, and so on.

00:47:45.517 --> 00:47:51.606
Now let's add our сonvolutions of convolutional coefficients

00:47:51.606 --> 00:47:57.729
plus the doubled list of convolution coefficients, but shifted.

00:47:59.186 --> 00:48:02.361
Or is it better vice versa...

00:48:02.361 --> 00:48:07.839
Twice the unshifted plus one shifted.

00:48:07.839 --> 00:48:12.287
We get 4, 9, 20, 40, 78...

00:48:12.287 --> 00:48:14.287
It matches!

00:48:14.287 --> 00:48:25.847
We have found that N times F of N
equals this convolution expression.

00:48:25.847 --> 00:48:29.522
By the way, another interesting observation
about the generating function...

00:48:29.522 --> 00:48:32.123
Let's do this.

00:48:32.123 --> 00:48:39.105
What is the series equal to at x equals 1?
Not x equals 0, but x equals 1?

00:48:40.000 --> 00:48:43.038
Here we see Fibonacci numbers again.

00:48:43.038 --> 00:48:47.451
Only every other one is missing...

00:48:47.451 --> 00:48:52.500
Every other Fibonacci number is missing 
 and the signs alternate.

00:48:52.500 --> 00:48:56.304
And at x equals -1,

00:48:56.304 --> 00:49:00.849
1, -1, 2, -3, 5, -8...

00:49:00.849 --> 00:49:05.426
Here they are not missing, but the signs alternate.

00:49:05.426 --> 00:49:09.273
What do you think follows from this?

00:49:09.273 --> 00:49:12.468
And finally...

00:49:12.468 --> 00:49:16.047
Let's draw the function 1 over G of x,

00:49:16.047 --> 00:49:27.913
and also draw the value of 
 this function G of x on the complex plane.

00:49:27.913 --> 00:49:35.840
Here we have two poles at the points...

00:49:35.840 --> 00:49:40.935
As far as I know, 1/φ and -φ (phi).

00:49:40.935 --> 00:49:45.211
This is negative phi on the real axis,

00:49:45.211 --> 00:49:50.000
and this is 1/φ.

00:49:53.997 --> 00:49:57.250
That's all for today. Stay tuned for bonus tracks!

00:49:57.250 --> 00:49:59.465
Goodbye, everyone!

